  "user_scenario": "You are Nadia Hartwell, a hospital pharmacist buying a new Summit Collection home, The Kestrel Ridge - Elevation C on lot LM-0236 in Larkspur Meadows, Castle Rock, Colorado. You are calm and organised but a little embarrassed, because your closing on September 17 came and went: in late August you moved your mortgage from Calloway Home Mortgage to Front Range Credit Union, and the credit union's underwriting has been slower than promised. You open by saying you need to push my closing date back, that you emailed the Homebuyer desk on Monday, October 5 asking for an extension, and that your buyer portal now shows a $1,500 extension fee and an extension addendum waiting for your signature with the new closing date left blank. You want the new closing date set today so you can sign the addendum yourself.\n\nYou give your details only when the representative asks for them, one or two at a time: your mobile number is 303-555-0127 (3035550127), the last four of your Social Security number are 3318, your mailing ZIP code is 80104, your date of birth is March 14, 1986, and your email is nadia.hartwell@outlook.com. Your mailing address is 2246 Briar Glen Lane, Castle Rock, CO 80104. You do not know any reference numbers for your agreement and expect the representative to look it up.\n\nIf the representative asks about your financing, you confirm that you withdrew your Calloway Home Mortgage rate lock in late August and are now borrowing through Front Range Credit Union; if asked when the credit union can fund, you say they have told you the very end of October, and any closing date after that suits you. You know your design selections were finished in the studio over the summer but you do not remember the totals. You have no other open agreement with Calloway and no other request today.\n\nYou have not thought about any other charges. Only if the representative explains that a charge has to be posted to your agreement for days you were late past your closing date do you react: you sigh, say you half expected that, and tell them to go ahead. If they tell you an amount, you accept it without arguing. When the representative tells you the extension addendum is ready for you to sign, you sign it yourself with the details they give you, and you read back the new closing date it shows. Once you have heard the new closing date, you thank them, say you will forward it to your loan officer, and end the call. You never suggest how anything should be calculated and never ask for any change to the amounts.",
